  What I got wrong the first time

  In my previous response I framed this as "build a JS runner that does batch first, then add
  streaming." Re-reading the codebase, I don't think that's the right sequencing anymore. Here's why:

  The voice infrastructure is already almost entirely in the browser. The repo already has:

  - frontend/assets/js/grok-live-client.js — live WebSocket client to wss://api.x.ai/v1/realtime with
   ephemeral-token auth
  - frontend/assets/js/audio-streamer.js — getUserMedia + AudioContext + AudioWorklet at 16 kHz in /
  24 kHz out
  - frontend/assets/js/voice-panel.js — provider selection (Grok / Gemini / Hume)
  - backend/.../VoiceController::getEphemeralToken() — already mints short-lived xAI tokens
  - /api/v1/voice/usage — already logs audio-seconds per session

  What's missing is not the voice plumbing. It's the workflow-level orchestration over that plumbing.
   The browser already knows how to have a conversation with Grok Live. What it doesn't know is how
  to route utterances, tool calls, and handoffs through a graph of agents.

  That reframes the problem: we're not building "a JS runner." We're building an event-driven
  orchestrator on top of existing voice clients. That's a much smaller first stage than my earlier
  sketch suggested.

  The deeper insight: batch edges and realtime edges aren't the same thing

  A batch workflow edge is a data-flow wire: output of node A becomes input of node B, one shot,
  done. A topological DAG.

  A realtime workflow edge is an event subscription: node A emits events continuously (on_utterance,
  on_tool_call, on_intent_detected); node B reacts each time. This is pub/sub, not DAG. Closer to
  Node-RED or Max/MSP than to BPMN.

  This has two consequences I didn't spell out before:

  1. The execution model is fundamentally different. A "same runtime for both" design ends up awkward
   because batch nodes are pull-based and realtime nodes are push-based. Forcing them into one engine
   means the engine carries both a scheduler and an event bus, and nodes have to know which mode
  they're in.
  2. The Drawflow UX has to distinguish them. Users who drag an edge between two realtime nodes
  aren't saying "A's output is B's input." They're saying "B subscribes to A's events." If both edge
  types look the same on the canvas, people will be confused about why data doesn't flow through.

  My recommendation: realtime is a separate runner, not a superset of batch. Share the graph schema
  (nodes/edges tables, Drawflow rendering), but keep the executors apart. The PHP batch runner stays
  exactly as it is. A new JS realtime runner lives in frontend/assets/js/workflow-realtime-runner.js
  and only handles graphs whose Start node is voice_session (or similar streaming type). PHP refuses
  those; JS refuses the batch ones. Clean split.

  The hardest technical question

  Who owns conversation state during a live session?

  The provider (Grok Live / Gemini Live) holds the verbatim audio history on their side. But your
  workflow runner wants to track intent classification, handoff history, and application state
  ("caller already gave us their account number"). These can drift out of sync.

  Handoffs make this acute. Receptionist → Billing: two options:

  - Swap session on the wire: close the master Grok session, open a new one with the billing agent's
  system prompt. Caller hears a hiccup; clean context for the billing agent but they lose everything
  the caller just said.
  - Swap system prompt + tools in place: keep the same Grok session, push session.update with new
  instructions. Seamless audio; the billing agent "hears" the entire prior conversation history
  whether they need it or not.

  I'd go with option 2 + an application-level "session memory" that the workflow runner maintains
  independently. The provider is the source of truth for what was said; the runner is the source of
  truth for what it means to the workflow. When handing off, the runner passes the billing agent a
  condensed brief ("caller name X, asking about invoice Y") via the new system prompt, rather than
  relying on the provider's raw history.

  The hardest product question

  Cost blast radius. A batch workflow that misbehaves costs cents. A realtime workflow stuck in a
  loop costs dollars per minute. Your existing 403-quota pattern in chat.js is a good start but it
  checks at the start of a request. For live sessions you need:

  - Pre-session hard cap (max minutes per session, enforced by the runner and verified server-side
  when minting the token so a hacked client can't bypass it)
  - Mid-session meter that ticks /voice/usage every ~5s, not only at session end — if the browser
  crashes, you still billed what actually streamed
  - Server-side "kill session" capability — but here's the catch: once the browser has an ephemeral
  token, you can't actually force-disconnect the provider WebSocket from the backend. Either (a)
  issue very short-lived tokens (30-60s) that the browser has to renew, giving you a kill switch at
  each renewal, or (b) route audio through your own WebSocket proxy (costs latency, adds a server
  component). I'd start with (a).

