⏺ The core UX problem in one sentence                                                                
                                                                                                   
  The batch editor is a DAG of data-flow arrows ("output of A becomes input of B"). The realtime     
  editor needs to be a statechart of event-triggered transitions ("when X happens to A, become B").  
  Those are different mental models, but the visual vocabulary — boxes on a canvas connected by      
  arrows — is identical. That's our lever: if users can recognize the shape, they can learn the new  
  semantics with one small cognitive shift, not a whole new tool.                                    

  Metaphors I considered and rejected

  Before landing on a recommendation, I weighed several options against the constraint "must feel    
  like the batch editor to someone who already knows it":                                     
                             
  - Node-RED style pub/sub — nodes subscribe to topics, wires carry events. Accurate but the wires   
  look identical to data wires, which is exactly the confusion we want to avoid.              
  - Zapier-style "when this, then that" cards — accessible for simple cases, but abandons the canvas
  entirely. Users who already like the Drawflow canvas would feel downgraded.                        
  - Swimlane timeline (tracks for agent / tool / user with events flowing left-to-right) — brilliant
  for debugging a completed session, but terrible for authoring because time isn't explicit in the   
  design.                                                                                            
  - Phone tree / org chart — perfect for the receptionist example, too rigid for anything else.
  - Stage with actors + side panel — good metaphor for "one voice at a time" but requires a          
  completely new interaction surface. Too much to learn.                                             
                                                                                              
  The recommendation: statechart inside a "session frame"                                            
                                                                                                     
  Keep the Drawflow canvas. Keep boxes and arrows. Change four things:                               
                                                                                              
  1. A visible frame represents "the call." Everything inside the frame happens during a single live 
  session. The frame has an entry point (caller connects) and an exit point (caller hangs up). This  
  is the single most important anchor — it gives users a container for "things that only make sense
  while the call is live," and it visually separates realtime concerns from the rest of the workflow.
  2. Agent nodes become states. Same rounded box as today, same palette drag-and-drop, but with a
  small mic icon indicating it's a voice persona. Exactly one agent is "active" at any moment during 
  a session (the one currently talking to the caller).                                        
  3. Arrows become labeled event transitions. Instead of unlabeled arrows meaning "data flows here,"
  realtime arrows have a required label: on intent:billing, on tool_call:transfer, on silence > 10s. 
  Visually they're dashed to distinguish from solid data-flow arrows in batch graphs. When the user
  drags an arrow between two agents, the editor opens a small inspector asking "what event triggers
  this handoff?" with a dropdown of known events — they can't create an unlabeled realtime arrow at  
  all. This makes the metaphor teach itself.
  4. Observers pin to the frame border. Cross-cutting nodes — a safety monitor, a transcript logger,
  a usage meter — don't fit anywhere in the state graph because they're "always on." They get a      
  special gutter along the top or side of the session frame labeled "Always listening." Conceptually
  clean; visually distinct from the state machine inside.
                                                                                                     
