  Here's where it gets interesting. Some batch workflows run in seconds — a simple DB lookup, a      
  single LLM synthesis. Fine for a live voice call; the agent says "one moment" and the result comes 
  back before the caller notices. But some batch workflows run for minutes — research tasks,
  multi-document analysis, long reasoning chains. You can't stall a voice call for two minutes       
  without the caller hanging up.

  So batch-workflow-as-tool needs two calling modes:                                                 
                                                                                                  
  Synchronous mode (for fast batch workflows): the tool call blocks until the workflow returns, same 
  as any other tool call. The runner should enforce a max timeout (say, 10 seconds) — anything longer
   gets upgraded to async mode automatically.                 
                                                                                                     
