  What this means for the editor

  When a user draws an arrow between two agents, the "what triggers this?" dialog should be grouped  
  by mechanism because that's what actually matters — it tells the user the cost, latency, and
  reliability of the event they're choosing:                                                         
                  
  How should this transition fire?
                                                                                                     
    ━━━ Free, instant, deterministic ━━━                                                             
    ○ User says a keyword / phrase         (Mechanism 1)                                             
    ○ Silence for N seconds                (Mechanism 2)                                             
    ○ Session / lifecycle signal           (Mechanism 2)
    ○ Timer elapsed                        (Mechanism 5)                                             
    ○ Tool result matches a condition      (Mechanism 4)                                             
                                                                                                     
    ━━━ Free, conversational ━━━                                                                     
    ○ Agent decides (emission tool call)   (Mechanism 3)                                             
                                                                                                     
    ━━━ Opt-in, costs extra ━━━                                                                      
    ○ Observer detects cross-cutting event (Mechanism 6)                                             
                                                                                                     
  Users learn, just from the picker, that the first group is cheap and reliable, the second group is 
  the default for anything semantic, and the third group should only be used when the first two can't
   catch what they need.                                                                             
                  
  The subtleties worth flagging now                                                                  
   
  A few things that will bite us if we ignore them:                                                  
                  
  Partial vs. final transcripts. Both Gemini and Grok stream partials that get revised. If we fire   
  keyword events on partials, we'll get spurious firings when the model corrects itself. Always gate
  text-based recognition on is_final. The cost is a small latency (a few hundred ms between partial  
  and final), but that's acceptable.

  Tool call validation. The LLM might emit a tool call with malformed arguments (wrong enum value,   
  missing required field). The runner must validate before firing the corresponding event. If
  validation fails, don't fire anything and optionally feed the error back to the LLM ("that intent  
  isn't valid, try again").

  Debouncing and commitment. Observer events especially need debounce — a classifier fluctuating     
  between 0.6 and 0.8 confidence shouldn't fire and un-fire in a loop. Once an event fires, it should
   have a cooldown before it can fire again. Probably configurable per-edge in the editor, with a    
  sensible default.

  Event ordering in a busy moment. What if in the same 100ms window, the LLM calls                   
  handoff_to(billing) AND the observer detects escalation AND a silence timer fires? Three events
  arrive at the runner nearly simultaneously. Which wins? The runner needs a priority model. My lean:
   observer events are highest priority (safety concerns trump domain logic), then LLM tool calls,
  then timers, then transcript matches. But this is a design choice worth being explicit about.

  State-dependent recognition. The same signal can mean different things in different states. "Yes"  
  after "Do you want to proceed?" means commit. "Yes" after "Are you upset?" means don't commit.
  Mechanism 3 handles this naturally because the LLM has context. Mechanisms 1 and 4 don't — they're 
  stateless. If a user wires a keyword-match event on "yes" at the graph level, they're going to get
  burned. The editor should probably steer users toward Mechanism 3 for anything semantic and only
  use Mechanism 1 for truly context-free keywords like "operator" or "human."

